    "body": "I have the following corresponding tests failing my local WebKit implementation:\r\nFAIL th.colSpan: setAttribute() to 2147483648 assert_equals: IDL get expected 1000 but got 1\r\nFAIL th.colSpan: setAttribute() to 4294967295 assert_equals: IDL get expected 1000 but got 1\r\nFAIL th.colSpan: setAttribute() to 4294967296 assert_equals: IDL get expected 1000 but got 1\r\nFAIL th.rowSpan: setAttribute() to 2147483648 assert_equals: IDL get expected 65534 but got 1\r\nFAIL th.rowSpan: setAttribute() to 4294967295 assert_equals: IDL get expected 65534 but got 1\r\nFAIL th.rowSpan: setAttribute() to 4294967296 assert_equals: IDL get expected 65534 but got 1\r\n\r\nWhen parsing an HTML integer in WebKit (following https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#rules-for-parsing-integers), if the parsed integer overflows an int variable, we throw an error in webKit. This is why we get the default value of 1 here, before we get a chance to clamp it.\r\n\r\nWhat is the expected behavior here? Is the test right? If so, are we supposed to parse HTML integers in a larger type than int, and then later clamp it either based on HTML clamping rules or IDL long / unsigned long range?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
